// Releasing `NO_HANDLE` is a fault.
//
//   SOS releasenothing: releasing a word that names nothing
//   SOS: process fault: bad handle process=0
//   SOS: process teardown handles=3 ... process=0
//
// Handle 0 names nothing (§3), which is why a zeroed register is never
// authority, so releasing it asks the kernel to destroy a capability the caller
// never had. That is caller-checkable in the strongest sense: the process chose
// the number. The kernel ends the process, and with the same `BadHandle` any
// other unresolvable word gets: `NO_HANDLE`, a malformed word and a stale one
// (see the sibling `handle-*` cases) are one answer, not three.
//
// The teardown count is the second assertion. Three handles, §12's boot set and
// nothing else, because this process deliberately derives nothing before it
// dies. That makes the number a statement about the fault, not about whatever
// the program happened to be holding.
//
// One fault per case, which is why this is not an arm of `handle-remint`: a
// fault ends the process, so a second probe after one would never run.

import sos.{System, sos_handle_release}

static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

/// The word that names nothing, at every generation. It is `sosabi`'s
/// `NO_HANDLE`, spelled here because this file works at the C altitude where a
/// handle is a number, and because the claim is that a process may write this
/// number and the kernel must refuse it.
static NAMES_NOTHING: UInt = 0

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS releasenothing: releasing a word that names nothing\n")

    // Does not return: the kernel ends this process for a handle that resolves
    // to no object.
    let _ = sos_handle_release(NAMES_NOTHING)

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS releasenothing: survived, which is the bug\n")
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}
